WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.300
Imagine you ask an AI assistant to
clean up an old project directory.

2
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.340
It seems like a simple request.

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:14.150
But when that assistant initiates a delete
command on your files, it forces the

4
00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.800
operating system to ask
a critical question.

5
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.080
Who actually authorized this?

6
00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.400
To the operating system, an AI
agent is just another process.

7
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.990
It runs a language model that proposes a
specific tool call, formatted as a name

8
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:32.570
with arguments, and relies on a paired
program to actually execute it.

9
00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:35.040
This creates a dangerous security gap.

10
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.020
The agent executes commands on behalf of
the user, but it is not the human user.

11
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.720
Its decisions come from a
statistical model predicting text.

12
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.230
That model can guess wrong and hallucinate,
or it can be actively manipulated by

13
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.760
hidden instructions in a document it reads,
an attack known as prompt injection.

14
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.390
Because the agent's decisions are inherently
untrustworthy, keeping its actions safe

15
00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.280
is strictly an operating
system security problem.

16
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.360
From the kernel's perspective, this
agent is a massive hardware workload.

17
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:09.610
The system routes inference calculations
to GPUs and NPUs using the Linux accel

18
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:14.730
subsystem. Before the model can even evaluate
the user's prompt, the system has to

19
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:17.520
load its huge read-only
weights into memory.

20
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.000
Linux handles this elegantly
with the mmap system call.

21
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:24.570
It reads the weights directly from the
page cache the moment they are touched,

22
00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:27.320
completely avoiding the need
to copy them into memory.

23
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.980
As the model processes the conversation,
it stores keys and values in a KV cache.

24
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.390
This memory footprint continuously grows
with the length of the conversation, so the

25
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.920
system doesn't have to
recompute past tokens.

26
00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.760
After crunching the math, the model
finalizes its proposal to delete the folder.

27
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.980
Now the operating system must
step in to intercept it.

28
00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:52.440
The architecture of an AI-native
OS relies on one absolute rule.

29
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.820
The model proposes, but the
operating system disposes.

30
00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:00.410
To enforce this rule, the kernel must
treat all output generated by the model

31
00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.300
strictly as untrusted external text.

32
00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.540
Windows addresses this by placing the
agent in a dedicated agent workspace.

33
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.870
The agent operates in parallel with the
human user, restricted to its own separate

34
00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.540
account. Microsoft execution containers
isolate the agent's processes.

35
00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.870
When the agent needs to access the file
system, it routes requests through model

36
00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.900
context protocol connectors, restricted
to the least privilege possible.

37
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.880
The entire Windows philosophy relies on
maintaining physical and logical separation,

38
00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:34.940
confining the agent inside parallel spaces
governed by strict declarative policies.

39
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:40.080
On x86-64 Linux, there is no
single system-wide agent.

40
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.970
Instead, distributions like Ubuntu combine
granular kernel primitives to construct a

41
00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:49.630
confined environment. The agent process
runs in user mode above the kernel's

42
00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.350
privilege boundary. First, namespaces
isolate the agent's perspective, limiting its

43
00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.680
view of mounts and users. Next,
cgroups cap CPU and memory consumption.

44
00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.300
Finally, seccomp filters block unapproved
system calls from crossing the boundary.

45
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.430
Before the agent can even attempt to
execute its tool call, Linux has already

46
00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.840
severely crippled its environment, its
hardware resources and its kernel access.

47
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.850
With its environment locked down, the
agent sends its pending folder deletion

48
00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:20.920
request over a Unix socket to a
background daemon like agentd.

49
00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:24.720
The daemon applies the first
layer of defense: a strict parser.

50
00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:29.050
If the request doesn't perfectly match a
closed catalog of authorized tool calls, it

51
00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:33.420
is instantly rejected. The second
layer is a strict path policy.

52
00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.950
The daemon restricts all file paths to the
internal workspace, resolves any symbolic

53
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.380
links and forbids relative escape
commands like double dots.

54
00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.070
Because deleting files is a destructive
action, the daemon triggers the third layer,

55
00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:49.320
pausing the execution to
demand explicit user consent.

56
00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:53.970
Looking at this terminal output, you can
see the model outputting a tool call, the

57
00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:58.220
internal policy allowing it and
the sandbox ready to enforce it.

58
00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:03.070
Even if that internal policy fails, a
kernel sandbox using Landlock acts as the

59
00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.600
fourth layer, creating an impenetrable
wall right at the privilege boundary.

60
00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:10.740
Finally, the fifth layer handles auditing.

61
00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:16.570
Every decision is logged to audit.log
using the SO_PEERCRED socket option to pull

62
00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:20.000
the true client UID and PID
directly from the kernel.

63
00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.300
This entire lifecycle illustrates
the principle of defense in depth.

64
00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.180
No single security layer trusts the
previous one functioned correctly.

65
00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:30.000
Let's finish with a quick review.

66
00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.050
Question one, what is an AI
agent to the operating system?

67
00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:38.770
Answer, it is a language model that proposes
tool calls plus a program that runs

68
00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:44.290
them. Question two, what system call loads
the model's huge read-only weights without

69
00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:47.680
copying them? Answer,
the mmap system call.

70
00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:54.680
Now open animations A9.1 and A9.3
in the course hub and run lab TD8.
